===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To follow or [[move]] in a [[wind]]ing route.
#: {{syn|en|slither|wind}}
#: {{ux|en|The path '''snaked''' through the forest.}}
#: {{ux|en|The river '''snakes''' through the valley.}}
#* {{quote-newsgroup|en
|title=Football fever...
|group=aus.personals
|author=Mark Addinall
|date=September 24, 1996
|passage=Any Brisbane female interested in '''snaking''' down a few beers whilst watching the footy on a big screen?
|url=https://groups.google.com/forum/?fromgroups=#!topic/aus.personals/WH4F4w5XpaQ}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2021 December 29|author=Stephen Roberts|title=Stories and facts behind railway plaques: Bournemouth (circa 1880)|journal=RAIL|issue=947|pages=59–60|text=Opened in June of that year [1880], the station was the southern terminus of the much-lamented Somerset and Dorset Joint Railway (the S&D  or 'Slow and Dirty'), which '''snaked''' its way down from Bath.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|Australia|slang}} To steal slyly.
#: {{ux|en|He '''snaked''' my DVD!}}
#* {{quote-newsgroup|en
|title=Home made supercharger ?
|group=aus.cars
|author=Hyena
|date=April 5, 2001
|passage=Although it wouldn't be the first time some one patented an idea that I'd had a year earlier.{{...}}Someone already has :){{...}}F*CK ME !!  '''Snaked''' again !
|url=https://groups.google.com/forum/?fromgroups=#!topic/aus.cars/fMo1P5elErs}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[clean]] using a plumbing snake.
# {{lb|en|US|informal}} To drag or draw, as a snake from a hole; often with ''out''.
#* '''November 27 1835''', N.B. St. John, ''letter to George Thompson''
#*: {{quote|en|his wife and children shall not be forced to flee from the hearth of a friend, lest they should be '''''snaked out''''' by men in civic authority}}
# {{lb|en|nautical}} To wind round spirally, as a large rope with a smaller, or with cord, the small rope lying in the spaces between the strands of the large one; to [[worm]].
# {{lb|en|MLE}} To inform; to [[rat]].
#: {{ux|en|He says he didn't '''snake''' and I believe him.}}

